# Document 3 — Product Philosophy

### The Core Filtering Question

Every single feature, button, or workflow proposed for Koinonia must pass one non-negotiable test before a single line of code is written:

> **"Does this feature make it easier for believers to gather, pray, and build genuine fellowship in Christ, or does it invite distraction and self-promotion?"**

If a feature encourages presence, active prayer, or shared study, it belongs in Koinonia. If it encourages vanity, passive scrolling, or friction, it gets rejected immediately.

---

### Product Rules & Design Laws

#### 1. Zero-Friction Gathering

* **Rule:** A user should be able to go from an invite link to sitting in an active prayer call or joining a fellowship group in 2 clicks maximum.
* **Why:** Spiritual momentum is fragile. High friction (forced complex setups, long onboarding forms, or multi-step room access) kills real-time participation.

#### 2. Sacred Fellowship Over Performance

* **Rule:** No vanity metrics. No public follower counts, no view counts, and no algorithmic trending feeds.
* **Why:** Spiritual gatherings are centered on God and mutual encouragement, not personal branding or clout.

#### 3. Prayer as an Active State, Not Passive Text

* **Rule:** Prayer requests must live as dynamic, trackable items within groups—not isolated text messages that get buried in a chat stream.
* **Why:** When a believer asks for prayer, the platform must make it seamless for others to intercede, acknowledge, and track answers to prayer over time.

#### 4. Seamless Across Web & Installed PWA

* **Rule:** The web application must be built progressive-first (PWA), offering native installability, fast load times, low-data performance, and push notifications on both desktop and mobile devices.
* **Why:** Believers gather globally across varying network conditions and hardware. Access must be instantaneous regardless of OS or app store barriers.

#### 5. Clean, Distraction-Free Interface

* **Rule:** Interface elements must stay out of the way during live meetings and quiet devotional reading. Dark/Light themes must support reverent focus.
* **Why:** The app is a conduit for spiritual connection, not a noisy digital hub competing for attention.

---

### What We Will Never Build (and Why)

* **Public "Explore" or Viral Feeds:** We will never build a TikTok-style or Instagram-style feed. Believers assemble in intentional groups, not algorithmic echo chambers.
* **Paid Tier Call-Duration Limits for Personal Groups:** We will never cut off an active prayer meeting abruptly after 40 minutes just to force a corporate upgrade.
* **Complex Multi-Role Hierarchy Systems:** We will not replicate Discord's complex permission trees. Simple group roles—**Host (Organizer)** and **Member (Participant)**—keep administration clear and lightweight.
* **Ad Networks & Retargeting Scripts:** We will never sell user space to third-party ad networks or insert banner ads inside prayer rooms or fellowship channels.

---

### The Marketing Page Philosophy (The Front Door)

Since Koinonia’s primary entry point for new users will be the web, the marketing landing page serves as the sacred "Front Door" of the application.

* **Clear Value Proposition above the Fold:** Visitors immediately understand what Koinonia is within 3 seconds: *"A dedicated space for Christian fellowship, audio/video prayer rooms, and Bible study groups."*
* **Interactive Live Preview / Demo Flow:** Potential group leaders can instantly preview how simple it is to launch a room or invite their group before even registering.
* **One-Click Installation:** Directly on the marketing site, visitors can click "Install App" (via PWA) or "Create a Gathering" to jump straight into the product.
* **Social Proof through Purpose:** Highlighting testimonials from intercessors, small group leaders, and global fellowships rather than corporate metrics.

