# Document 5 — Technical Architecture

Since Koinonia does not rely on generative AI, this document outlines the core system design, data flows, client architecture, media routing, and infrastructure scaling.

---

### System Architecture Overview

```
[ Progressive Web App (PWA) / Web Client ]
       |                              |
       | (REST / Realtime WS)         | (WebRTC Audio/Video/Screen)
       v                              v
[ Supabase Backend ]            [ LiveKit / WebRTC SFU ]
  ├── Auth (JWT & Magic Links)    ├── Selective Forwarding Unit
  ├── Postgres Database (RLS)     ├── Audio / Video Rooms
  └── Realtime (WebSockets)       └── Screen Share Stream
       |
       | (Media Storage / Optimization)
       v
[ Cloudinary ]
  ├── User Avatars
  ├── Group Cover Images
  └── Chat Attachments & Documents

```

---

### Key Tech Stack Components

| Layer | Technology | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Frontend Platform** | Next.js 16 / React (PWA) | Progressive Web App installed on Desktop/Mobile with Service Workers, Web Push, and Offline Shell. |
| **Backend & Auth** | Supabase | Managed PostgreSQL, Row-Level Security (RLS), Auth (Email/Password, Magic Link), and Realtime WebSockets for instant chat/reactions. |
| **Live Audio/Video/Screen** | LiveKit (or Agora SFU) | Multi-party WebRTC audio/video rooms, active speaker detection, low-latency screen sharing, and adaptive bitrate streaming. |
| **Media & Asset Management** | Cloudinary | Dynamic image optimization, user avatar transformations, document upload handling, and CDN delivery for attachments. |
| **Hosting & Edge Delivery** | Vercel / Cloudflare | Global CDN delivery, low latency edge routing, SSL, and custom domain routing. |

---

### Data Isolation & Privacy Security Model (Supabase RLS)

All data access is strictly governed at the database level using Supabase Row-Level Security (RLS):

1. **Group & Channel Isolation:** Users can only read/write messages in groups where they hold an active membership record in `group_members`.
2. **Prayer Board Privacy:** Prayer requests marked as "Private / Group Only" are constrained to that group ID via database policies.
3. **Meeting Access Control:** LiveKit join tokens are generated via serverless backend endpoints that verify the user’s authenticated JWT and active group membership before issuing room access.

---

### Real-Time Infrastructure (Chat, Audio/Video & Reactions)

#### 1. Low-Latency Audio/Video (LiveKit SFU)

* **Selective Forwarding Unit (SFU):** Instead of peer-to-peer (Mesh), video and audio streams route through an SFU server, allowing low-bandwidth multi-party calls (up to 100+ participants per room).
* **Audio-First Bandwidth Optimization:** Audio prayer rooms automatically disable video tracks to ensure crystal-clear audio quality even on low 2G/3G mobile networks.
* **Screen Sharing:** High-framerate stream encoding dedicated for Bible study slide decks, document reading, and scripture passages.

#### 2. Chat & Interactive Reactions (Supabase Realtime)

* **Realtime WebSockets:** Chat messages, live meeting reactions (Amen, Praying Hands), and "I Prayed for This" counter updates broadcast over persistent Supabase Realtime channels with sub-100ms latency.
* **Persistent History:** Chat messages and pinned scripture notes persist directly to PostgreSQL for asynchronous access after calls end.

---

### Media Storage Pipeline (Cloudinary)

* **Upload Presets:** Client uploads media directly to Cloudinary using signed upload parameters generated by Supabase Edge Functions.
* **Optimization Pipeline:** Auto-converts avatars and group graphics to lightweight `.webp` formats with responsive transformations, preserving user bandwidth.

---

### Performance & PWA Installation Strategy

* **PWA Service Worker:** Caches application shell, static assets, and UI components locally for instant app loading.
* **Web Push Notifications:** Integrated with the browser Push API to alert users when scheduled prayer times begin or when someone responds to a prayer request.

